## Summary Nodemailer's SMTP client applies four regular expressions to a peer's EHLO response to detect the supported SASL mechanisms. Each pattern uses an alternation with two overlapping whitespace-matching sub-patterns, which produces quadratic (O(n^2)) backtracking whenever the mechanism name is absent from the line. A malicious or MITM SMTP server that answers EHLO with `250-AUTH` followed by a long whitespace-only run and a non-matching token blocks the Node.js event loop for tens of seconds to hours during a pre-authentication handshake. No authentication to the target SMTP server is required, and any application that connects to an attacker-influenced host is affected. ## Root Cause Each of the four AUTH mechanism regexes in `SMTPConnection._actionEHLO` contains the sub-pattern `(?:(\s+|=)[^
]*\s+|\s+|=)` before the mechanism literal. `[^
]*` and the trailing `\s+` both match whitespace and can consume the same characters, so when the mechanism literal is absent, the regex engine must try every partition of the whitespace run between `[^
]*` and `\s+` before failing. The four regexes run unconditionally on every EHLO response, with no length cap, no shape validation, and no early rejection before the engine enters the ambiguous alternation. ```text Component: nodemail
